# Voice performance: the DVTD stationary flow rig on subject 1's /sh/ (s1-12-schoen-sh) at the printed 3.0046 W
#   level: P_sub 3115.9 Pa at the tap; printed comparison values U 0.00096429 m^3/s and SPL 77.2 dB (the file's
#   reference reads 2*10e-5 Pa; its printed PSD integrates to 97.22 dB re 20 uPa).
# Card by OliveDove, fric1 (owner order MB 15368; GO 19580, clock 19667; the /s/ rig card's rulings 15490, 15773,
#   15828, 15902, 16025, 16735, 17645, 17712, 18252; NavyTower 15491, 15765, 16041). Every number below is read
#   from a cited file; none is fitted to a recording.
#
# SOURCE OF THE RIG: P. Birkholz et al. (2020), Sci. Data 7:255, doi 10.1038/s41597-020-00597-w (CC BY 4.0), and
#   its dataset (the DVTD archive, CC0, figshare 11897187). The printed values and the PSD comparison file are
#   s1-12-schoen-sh-noise-metadata.txt and s1-12-schoen-sh-noise-psd.txt of that archive.
# TRACT: s1-12-schoen-sh-inner-surface-closed.stl cut by the level route (slice3d.py, NavyTower): 102 sections
#   of about 2 mm, glottis first. Section 0 = 1.179573e-04 m^2. Rigid (no tract_wall row): a printed solid.
# FRICATIVE SOURCE: Stevens' printed source at an obstacle (K. N. Stevens, Acoustic Phonetics, Fig. 2.33(a), on
#   Shadle's 1985 data; read on MIT OCW 6.541J handout 03/02/04, p. 6; MB 17645, 17712): in 300 Hz bands
#   L = C(f) + 20 log10(U^3 A^-2.5) - 212 dB re 1 dyn/cm^2, U in cm^3/s and A in cm^2, C(f) the upper curve as
#   digitized in fs-couple's turbulence_noise.rs (0.55-10 kHz). The caption's U^3 rule is used as printed: the
#   figure's own two curves imply U^1.89 to U^2.78, and no exponent is refit. A is the constriction k = 78
#   (9.343011e-06 m^2, the cut's minimum), opening into j = 101 (7.787694e-04 m^2, the largest section between k
#   and the lips, MB 15828), whose Borda-Carnot drop is solved in the glottal junction with Shadle's measured K3
#   less the line's own Poiseuille resistance over the sections the constriction spans (MB 18650, 19370); seed 1.
#   The source sits at the obstacle m = 92, the junction after section 92, 28.7 mm downstream of k along
#   the cut: the incisors' gap, measured on the same surface as the waist between the cavity behind the teeth and
#   the cavity behind the lips on 11 midsagittal slices (its edges lie in sections 91 to 94, their median in
#   section 92). The printed curve is the source in Shadle's tube (2.54 cm across, 5.067 cm^2; RLE TR 506, p. 27);
#   Shadle's eq. 3.3 (p. 67) makes the same dipole a pressure source inversely proportional to its duct's area:
#   the law multiplies it by A_tube / A_m, A_m section 92's 1.678607e-04 m^2, +9.60 dB (MB 18252). The
#   figure's rig had one 0.0804 cm^2 constriction, its obstacle 3 cm downstream, a 5.0 cm^2 tube and flows of
#   160 and 420 cm^3/s; this rig's printed 466 to 964 cm^3/s through 0.093 cm^2 lie above
#   those flows: the gap is stated on the page.
# GLOTTIS: the model's own adapter hole (the article: a 4 mm ring, inner diameter 10 mm, flush with the glottal
#   plane). The printable file's bore measures 78.096 mm^2 (9.97 mm), the mean of 9 plane
#   sections, over 3.77 mm: its bottom face meets the bore 3.773 mm below the glottal plane on
#   average. glottal_orifice takes Ishizaka and Flanagan (1972) eq 6 recovery into section 0, N = 0.6621
#   (MB 16025). The fold rows are unread (the grammar needs them; glottal_orifice replaces the folds).
# SUBGLOTTAL TUBE, glottis end first, from the printed misc parts by plane sections: the 3.77 mm hole;
#   trachea.stl's bore, 253.852 mm^2 (18 mm), 82.0 mm (its 5.0 mm spigot fills the horn's 5.0 mm
#   thread socket); bronchial_horn.stl's bore from the socket floor down 17 slices of 2.0 mm and one of
#   0.23 mm, each at its midpoint's area, to 120.0 mm below the glottal plane, the article's pressure tap (no
#   tap port is in either printed part). The article's 18 to 10 mm taper is in no printed part; the card follows
#   the files (MB 15828). Below the tap the horn and the tank are replaced by the held pressure.
# SUBGLOTTAL PRESSURE: the printed P_sub, held at the tube's far end (the tap). The 0.1 s ramp is authored and
#   lies before the reads (the last 2 s).
# AMBIENT: 22 C, 1007 hPa, 46 % relative humidity, as the article prints for the anechoic chamber.
# TERMINATION: the Levine-Schwinger exact unflanged thin-wall load, 256 quadrature nodes (MB 15902). The printed
#   wall is 4 mm thick: the real load lies between the unflanged and flanged limits.
# RECEIVER: the mouth flow as a free-field monopole at the rig microphone, 0.30 m in front of and 0.30 m beside the
#   mouth (0.424 m), as the article prints; the 45-degree directivity is not modelled.
# AUDIO: 26 kHz (MB 19667), 3.0 s, one mechanics step per output sample. The lip section (7.787694e-04
#   m^2, a = 15.745 mm) has its Levine-Schwinger cut-on (ka 3.8317) at 13370 Hz (c 345.19 m/s, Cramer
#   1993), so Nyquist must lie below it; the /s/ rig cards run at 40 kHz. Microphone band 10400 Hz, 0.4 of the clock.
# FULL SCALE: full_scale_pa 4.0 Pa scales the PCM16 WAV only and enters no physics (MB 25233). The value is set
#   from the measured peaks of the first renders (2.273 Pa at 3005 mW, about 4.9 dB of headroom), not from the
#   recording. The first cards carried the /s/ rig cards' 1.0 and clipped from 1.5 W: samples at the PCM16 rails, of
#   78000, on voice_rig-549e2a (ts2, 2026-11-11): 501 mW 0, 1000 mW 0, 1501 mW 28, 2002 mW 601, 2499 mW 2344,
#   3005 mW 5092.
frankensim-voice-performance-v1
audio 26000 78000 1
full_scale_pa 4.0
ambient moist_air 295.15 100700 0.46
fold two_mass 1.25e-4 2.5e-5 80 8 25 0.1 3.57e-4 1.4e-2 2.5e-3
fold_damping_upper 0.6
termination unflanged_thin_wall 256
